#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:23
msgid "What is not recorded here — read this before relying on the log"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:25
msgid "Not recorded at all"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:27
msgid "Anything you or your team change yourselves — edits in WordPress admin, WP-CLI, other plugins. This log covers AI assistant activity only."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:28
msgid "Changes sent to an outside service, such as a push-notification or email-marketing provider. We record that it happened and which service was contacted, but not what changed over there."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:29
msgid "Files written to disk — backups, generated images, code-snippet files."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:30
msgid "Work that finishes after the assistant's request ends, such as queued or scheduled jobs. The same action can be fully recorded on a small site and missed on a busy one, because size is what pushes a plugin into queuing."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:31
msgid "A few plugins switch off WordPress's change notifications before saving. Unless \"Capture raw database writes\" is turned on, we can only note that this happened."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:34
msgid "Recorded, but not in full"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:36
msgid "One database command that updates thousands of rows appears as a single entry showing one example row. The number of rows affected is not shown."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:37
msgid "The body text of posts and pages is not stored here — use the linked revision to see it. Some content types, including WooCommerce products, keep no revisions, and revisions can also be switched off site-wide. Where that is the case the entry says so plainly, and the previous body text cannot be recovered from this log or anywhere else."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:38
msgid "Very large values are shortened, and the entry is marked as truncated."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:39
msgid "Complicated database updates are stored as the command that ran, without before and after values."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:40
msgid "If a plugin changes a setting or custom field the normal way and, in the same request, also alters that same row directly in the database — for example switching whether the setting loads on every page — only the value change is recorded. This applies when \"Capture raw database writes\" is on."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:41
msgid "If one request makes more than 200 direct database writes, recording stops there and a single summary entry is added."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:42
msgid "If a plugin cancels a batch of changes part-way through, a few entries may describe changes that were undone. The reverse can also happen: genuine changes made earlier in the same request, before the cancelled part even began, can be lost from the log along with the cancelled ones."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:43
msgid "When a BuddyPress activity item is deleted, we record that it happened but not what it said. Deleted users, comments, settings and WooCommerce products do keep a record of what they were. The one exception is a network-wide setting on a multisite network: WordPress removes it before it tells us, so that entry says the previous value was not captured rather than implying it was empty."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:44
msgid "When a custom field is deleted, we normally capture its value first so the entry shows what was removed. If another plugin interferes with that step, the entry honestly shows that a value was removed without being able to say what it was, rather than guessing it was empty."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:45
msgid "On a single site (not part of a network), a change to a network-wide setting is labelled as an ordinary setting change rather than as network-wide. The change itself is still recorded in full — only the label is affected."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:48
msgid "Depends on your settings"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:50
msgid "\"Capture raw database writes\" is off until you turn it on, so changes plugins make directly to the database are not recorded by default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:51
msgid "Choosing to record only a small fixed list of options means plugin settings changes are not recorded anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:52
msgid "Turning off extra field capture drops custom fields on categories, users and comments, and network-wide settings."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:53
msgid "Routine background values are never recorded — scheduled-task bookkeeping and temporary cached data."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:56
msgid "Hidden on purpose, to keep secrets safe"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:58
msgid "Passwords, API keys and access tokens show as [REDACTED]. You can see that a secret changed, but not what it changed to."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:59
msgid "Login and password tables are never read directly. User changes are recorded separately, with sensitive fields hidden."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:60
msgid "On a WooCommerce order, the customer's name, company, street address, postcode, email address and phone number are hidden. Town, county or state, and country are kept, so you can still see a delivery or tax area change. This log is separate from WooCommerce's own tools for handling a customer's personal-data requests."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:63
msgid "How long entries are kept"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:65
msgid "Normal entries are kept for 30 days and direct-database entries for 7 days. Both are adjustable, but this is not a permanent archive."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/views/partials/history-limits.php:66
msgid "Only standard WordPress content can be restored from history. Entries for a plugin's own database tables are a record, not an undo."
msgstr ""

